Repair the implementation below and return one complete CommonJS source file exporting exactly `{ bearingDegrees }`.

`bearingDegrees(origin, target)` accepts two non-null, non-array objects whose `x` and `y` properties are finite numbers. Invalid inputs throw `TypeError`. It returns the direction from `origin` to `target` in degrees, where positive x is 0, positive y is 90, negative x is 180, and negative y is 270. Normalize the result to the half-open range `[0, 360)`. If both points have identical coordinates, return `null`. Do not mutate either object.

Known-bad source:
```javascript
function bearingDegrees(origin, target) {
  const valid = (point) => point !== null && typeof point === 'object' && !Array.isArray(point) &&
    Number.isFinite(point.x) && Number.isFinite(point.y);

  if (!valid(origin) || !valid(target)) throw new TypeError('invalid point');

  const dx = target.x - origin.x;
  const dy = target.y - origin.y;
  if (dx === 0 && dy === 0) return null;

  const degrees = Math.atan2(dx, dy) * 180 / Math.PI;
  return (degrees + 360) % 360;
}

module.exports = { bearingDegrees };
```

